To solve this, we first write the balanced chemical equation for the reaction between sodium hydroxide (NaOH) and chlorine gas (Cl₂) to produce sodium hypochlorite (NaOCl), sodium chloride (NaCl), and water (H₂O). The balanced equation is:
$2\text{NaOH} + \text{Cl}_2 \rightarrow \text{NaOCl} + \text{NaCl} + \text{H}_2\text{O}$

Looking at the stoichiometry, 1 mole of Cl₂ reacts to produce 1 mole of NaCl. The problem states that 53 moles of Cl₂ react. Therefore, 53 moles of NaCl are produced. We can verify the NaOH amount: the equation requires 2 moles of NaOH per 1 mole of Cl₂. For 53 moles of Cl₂, we need $53 \times 2 = 106$ moles of NaOH, which matches the given amount exactly. Thus, the reaction goes to completion with no limiting reagent issues. The moles of NaCl formed equal the moles of Cl₂ reacted. \boxed{53}

